5. Test your agent with these 8 questions

Don’t start by asking broad questions such as “Analyse everything.” Test the agent with focused questions and check whether its answers match your Excel data.

Test	Question to enter	What you should validate
1	How many problem records are available in the supplied dataset?	Whether the agent can establish the correct record count
2	Which business services appear in the dataset?	Whether it identifies services accurately
3	Show me the historical problems related to LOTS report generation.	Whether the returned problem numbers match the source
4	What root cause categories appear most frequently?	Whether the categories and counts are correct
5	Identify potentially related problems and explain why they may be connected.	Whether it cites actual records and distinguishes hypotheses from facts
6	Which problem categories may benefit from automated health checks?	Whether its recommendations are relevant to the recorded failures
7	Which production problems could benefit from additional regression testing?	Whether it maps problem types to sensible QA controls
8	Prepare an executive summary of the most important findings in this dataset.	Whether the summary is evidence-based and acknowledges limitations

What if the agent gives incorrect counts?

This is an important possibility.

An agent that retrieves information from Excel may be useful for finding relevant records, but retrieval alone does not guarantee accurate aggregation across all 500 rows.

If the agent cannot reliably calculate counts or percentages, the next step is to add a structured analytical capability—for example, a Power Automate flow, a suitable data query, or a Python-based analysis process approved by your organisation.

The calculation should be performed by that component, with the agent explaining the results.

